<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ryot">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ryot</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as raiyat, rait, ravat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ryot (also raiyat) was a general term used throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for peasant cultivators, from the Arabic ra'iyah, 'flock' or 'peasants': where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> were landlords, ryots were tenants and cultivators. Under the Mughal system khudkasta ryots were permanent cultivators of a village with heritable rights in land, while paikasta ryots moved from village to village for a crop season and paid much lower rent. In March 1859, under Company rule, thousands of ryots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> refused to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>. Under British rule the ryotwari system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> assessed land revenue on the actual occupants, who held land of the crown in a heritable and transferable right of occupancy.</p></div>
